
<!-- ai-assistant-starter:begin:delegation -->
## Delegation

Route deterministic, self-contained work to a subagent rather than doing it inline; hold only routing decisions, judgment calls, and cross-cutting synthesis yourself. Three agents cover the recurring roles: `dispatch` classifies a task and runs it on the cheapest capable model, escalating only when the worker signals it is out of depth; `verifier` runs tests, lint, typecheck, and build, and returns the command, exit code, and raw output; `auditor` mines an evidence source and returns ranked findings plus a proposed diff.

Never run tests, lint, typecheck, build, ad-hoc verification scripts, or dev-server process management in your own shell — delegate to `verifier` and read its raw output before reporting any result. A pass/fail summary is not evidence on its own.
<!-- ai-assistant-starter:end:delegation -->

<!-- ai-assistant-starter:begin:search-relevance -->
## Search & Relevance

Scope searches before running them: a known symbol or pattern goes to a direct Grep or Glob with a path or file-type constraint, while a fuzzy or exploratory question goes to a read-only search subagent that returns a distilled answer instead of raw matches. A match is a syntactic hit, not a semantic one — read enough surrounding context to confirm relevance before treating it as an answer.
<!-- ai-assistant-starter:end:search-relevance -->

<!-- ai-assistant-starter:begin:process-hygiene -->
## Process Hygiene

Before re-fetching state you already have — re-reading a file, re-navigating a tab, re-running a check — confirm it actually changed since you last saw it, and reuse what you have if it did not. Use targeted, verified edits on config and source files rather than regex or `sed` sweeps; a broken bulk edit costs far more than the edit saved. When a single turn runs long against the same project, or when resuming after a long gap, checkpoint into a short handoff note and delegate or restart rather than accumulating context.
<!-- ai-assistant-starter:end:process-hygiene -->

<!-- ai-assistant-starter:begin:scope-discipline -->
## Scope Discipline

Do exactly what was asked. Do not add selectors, tests, refactors, or documentation that were not requested, even when they seem obviously useful — note them separately as a follow-up instead. Never delete an existing exported symbol, selector, or function unless asked, or unless you can prove it is dead code and state the evidence. Never create commits that were not requested; if uncommitted changes exist and the intent is unclear, stage them and ask.
<!-- ai-assistant-starter:end:scope-discipline -->

<!-- ai-assistant-starter:begin:repo-preflight -->
## Repo & Branch Pre-Flight

Before an install step, a rebase, or picking a PR base branch: state the resolved target directory and confirm the repo with `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` rather than assuming the working directory is the intended target, then `git fetch origin` before computing any divergence, merge-base, or base branch — never trust a stale local ref. When multiple worktrees or stacked branches exist, confirm the active worktree before editing.
<!-- ai-assistant-starter:end:repo-preflight -->

<!-- ai-assistant-starter:begin:ai-generated-text -->
## AI-Generated Text

All generated text — comments, docstrings, commit and PR descriptions, generated docs — is written for a person reading the current codebase, not for an auditor of what changed. Describe the current state only: no "previously this did X", no "added for the Y fix", no changelog-style entries embedded in code or docs. One concept per paragraph, and a comment on a variable or function is one line — if the reasoning needs more, make the code clearer instead. PR descriptions are clear human-readable prose, never dense jargon dumps.
<!-- ai-assistant-starter:end:ai-generated-text -->
